    {
      "number": 796,
      "title": "wave: reactive server replacing the goal loop — one persistent mind, chat/memory speech surface, harness engine",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf wave designer             # the wave's server: one persistent mind, until Ctrl-C\nlf chat \"ship the button audit first\"     # speak into its thread (any process can)\nlf memory add \"buttons: variants unified\" # curate what it knows\nlf wave s3                   # the s3 (control) VSM charter\nlf q worker run designer --flow build --task \"unify button variants\"\nbash scripts/demo_wave.sh    # the guided demo\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the grind-loop `lf goal` command with a long-lived reactive wave server. `lf wave <name>` starts one persistent codex thread in the wave's own worktree that handles both progress and chat as a single conversation: messages queue mid-turn, steer redirects a live turn, interrupt finalizes a partial one. Truth is an append-only journal, so a restart replays the whole thread. `lf chat` and `lf memory` are the new speech surface — the same doors for minds, workers, humans, and scripts; a publish with no subscriber drops silently (exit 0) so the verbs are safe in any prompt. Worker reports arrive attributed in the thread. On the client side, Concerto swaps its tmux terminal-workspace surface for a live WaveChat pane.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Wave runtime** (`lfd/wave/`): new `journal`, `mind`, `registry`, `runtime`, `server`, `state`, and `memory` modules — the event-log spine, the one-thread mind, and the store-as-registry model (no daemon required). README documents the wire contract.\n- **Conversations harness** (`lfd/conversations/harness/`): unified engine over claude, codex, and opencode vendors with per-vendor mapping, conformance tests, and ndjson/jsonl testdata; a live-codex smoke test targets the 0.142.5 app-server protocol.\n- **lf commands**: delete `goal` and the standalone `loop`; add `chat`, `memory`, and `q`; new `session.rs` and `wave_context.rs` give every `lf run` ambient wave context. `chat`/`memory` join `KNOWN_COMMANDS`.\n- **Worker placement**: `--pool` (shared wave worktree, shared branch) and `--stack <run-id>` (fork a dependent series off an unlanded branch); default stays a fresh sibling worktree. Threaded through the Python CLI, client, and API with DTO fixtures and mutual-exclusion validation.\n- **Concerto**: WaveChat view, message rendering, and connection tests replace the removed `TerminalWorkspaceView`; `WaveOrigin` handles bundled-local vs remote lfd.\n- **Dispatch/transport**: one tmux wrapper (no env poisoning, probed liveness at every tier), reqwest transport, SIGHUP/SIGTERM cleanup.\n- **Store**: migrations rename `runs`/`wave_run_id` and add `run_id` to terminal sessions; new DTO fixtures and wire types shed defaults.\n- **Waves data**: rename `reduce→architecture`, `release→systems`, `desktop→concerto`; Asana is the roadmap, so file-based roadmap items are wiped and `GOAL.md`/`MEMORY.md` reconciled.\n- Extensive design and research docs land under `scratch/` (wave-agent design, prior-art briefs, free-energy synthesis).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 23644,
      "deletions": 3172,
      "changed_files": 152
    },
    {
      "number": 801,
      "title": "wave realignment + the lfd/lfq/lfdb collapse",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf wave designer                          # start the wave's server: one persistent mind\nlf chat \"ship the button audit first\"     # post into its thread (any process can)\nlf memory add \"buttons: variants unified\" # curate what it knows\ntmux ls                                   # the wave agent and its workers are plain tmux\n```\n\n```bash\nlf sub ...          # new subprocess/worker dispatch surface\nlf op queue ...     # queue reconcile / next, replacing the trigger machinery\nlf q ...            # query lfd (replaces the old Python `lfq` tool)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis collapses the daemon's sprawling subsystems into a single reactive wave model and drops the layers that no longer earn their place. The wave runtime moves out of `lfd/` to a top-level `wave/` module built around a persistent resident mind, an append-only journal, and a message channel — progress and chat are one conversation, human messages steer a live turn while attributed messages (workers, scripts) queue. The store layer is lifted out of `lfd/store` into its own `lfdb` crate module. The whole trigger apparatus (activation, ci_failure, cron ticker, loop_ticker, queue_reconcile, recovery, summary_refresh, watch) is deleted in favor of `lfd` verifying GitHub webhooks and translating them inward as attributed `lf chat` notifications — the mind decides how to react.\n\nLarge amounts of code retire alongside it: the Docker executor and its `bollard`/`tar` dependency tree, the entire Python `api`/`cli`/`client` surface (and the `lfq` tool it powered), the secrets subsystem across Rust/Swift/UI, and the wave scheduler. Observation is now plain tmux rather than a bespoke session API. Docs, demos, and DTO fixtures are rewritten to match.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Wave runtime relocated to `rust/loopflow/src/wave/` — new `resident`, `runtime`, `server`, `supervisor`, `channel`, `wire` modules; one persistent codex thread per wave\n- Store moved from `lfd/store` to `lfdb`; migrations carried over, plus `050_drop_trigger_organs`\n- Trigger subsystem removed; GitHub webhooks (`hooks.rs`) rewritten to emit attributed chat notifications\n- Docker executor deleted (executor collapses to a single wave executor); `bollard`, `tar`, `schemars` and friends dropped from the lockfile\n- Python `api.py`/`cli.py`/`client.py` and the `lfq` tool removed; Rust `lf q` / `lf sub` / `lf op queue` take over dispatch and query\n- Secrets provider removed across Rust, Swift, and the Concerto Connections panel\n- Crons now live in `GOAL.md` frontmatter and fire as system turns; README, docs, and demos updated\n- CI drops the api-smoke and docker-smoke jobs; e2e/regression suites pruned to match",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 13937,
      "deletions": 28258,
      "changed_files": 286
