    {
      "number": 802,
      "title": "lf op: plan-first rebase and worktree placement",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op rebase --plan          # show the reset/rebase strategy without touching git\nlf op rebase                 # apply the planned update\nlf op rebase origin/main     # rebase onto an explicit target\n\nlf op wt create my-feature --plan          # print the placement plan only\nlf op wt create child --stack parent       # create parent.child\nlf op wt create my-feature --main          # force a root branch from main\nlf op wt create feature --fork             # independent root from the review base\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nBoth `lf op rebase` and `lf op wt create` now classify the branch and print a plan before mutating git. Rebase distinguishes disposable branches (reset to base), stack children (rebase onto parent), and authored work (normal rebase), stashing `scratch/` under `.lf/scratch-stash/` when a reset would otherwise blow it away and restoring it afterward. Worktree creation is modeled as an explicit placement request — root, main, stack, or fork — that resolves to a strategy (create root, create stack child, checkout existing, reuse worktree) you can inspect with `--plan`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `PlacementRequest`/`PlacementPlan`/`PlacementStrategy` types in `worktrees.rs`; `plan_placement` and `create_from_placement_plan` split planning from execution.\n- `WorktreeSegment`/`StackBranch` newtypes enforce that dots are reserved for stack ancestry — use `--stack` for parent/child, hyphens within a segment.\n- `--plan`, `--main`, `--fork`, and optional-parent `--stack` flags on `lf op wt create`; `--plan` and explicit-target support on `lf op rebase`.\n- Collapsed `RebasePlan.scratch_stash` to a bool and dropped unwired enum variants.\n- Regression test for leading-space porcelain path parsing; e2e test `tests/e2e/test_rebase_efficiency.sh`; updated `docs/lfop.md`, `LOOPFLOW.md`, and goldens.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1207,
      "deletions": 72,
      "changed_files": 21
    },
    {
      "number": 803,
      "title": "compress: drop postgres backend, lf q command, and docker lfd path",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf build \"unify button variants\" --wave designer --dispatch\nlf implement \"Add the endpoint\" --wave engbot --dispatch\n```\n\nWorker dispatch now goes through the flow commands (`lf build/implement/... --wave <name> --dispatch`) instead of the removed `lf q worker run`. Same wave semantics — workers inherit the wave's `GOAL.md` and `MEMORY.md` and report back via PRs.\n\n## Summary\n\nA compression pass that removes three parallel code paths that no longer earn their keep. The Postgres `lfdb` backend is gone (SQLite is the single store), the `lf q` command surface is deleted in favor of flow-native dispatch, and the Docker/compose lfd service path is dropped in favor of direct per-OS service dispatch. Alongside the deletions, the conversation/harness plumbing moves out of `lfd` to the top level so it's no longer coupled to the daemon. Net ~4,300 lines removed.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Drop Postgres backend**: delete `lfdb/postgres.rs` and collapse `lfdb/mod.rs`, `catalog.rs`, `migrations.rs`, and `rows.rs` onto SQLite only. Removes the `deadpool-postgres`/`tokio-postgres`/`postgres-protocol`/`postgres-types` dependency tree (and its transitive `whoami`, `parking_lot`, `md-5`, etc.) from `Cargo.lock`.\n- **Remove `lf q`**: delete `lf/commands/q.rs` (~690 lines) and its wiring; workers now dispatch through the flow commands with `--wave`/`--dispatch`. README loses the `q worker run` examples and the `manual`/`loop` modes table.\n- **Drop docker lfd path**: delete `lfd/service/compose.rs` and fold service management into direct `cfg`-gated macOS/Linux dispatch; slim `scripts/concerto-dev.py` accordingly.\n- **Lift conversation plumbing out of `lfd`**: move `lfd/conversations/{turns,types}` into `chat/`, and the `harness/*` (claude/codex/opencode mappings, testdata, opencode runtime) to a top-level `harness` module. Move `wave_config` from `lfd/http/routes` into `engine`.\n- **Housekeeping**: shrink `lfd/config.rs`, add `wave/subscription.rs`, refresh docs (`lfd.md`, `waves.md`, `wave-authoring.md`, architecture) and goals `wave/goals` MEMORY, and update goldens/DTO fixtures to match.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1532,
      "deletions": 5880,
      "changed_files": 120
    },
    {
      "number": 804,
      "title": "release: v0.10.0",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ngit show v0.10.0:RELEASE_NOTES.md\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nCuts the v0.10.0 release. Bumps the version to `0.10.0` across `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock`, `pyproject.toml`, and `uv.lock`, and finalizes the release notes. This is the release where the **Wave** becomes loopflow's core primitive — a persistent, goal-driven looping agent that reads one `goal.md`, keeps its own `MEMORY.md`, works an Asana roadmap, and dispatches steerable sub-sessions. Around it the runtime collapsed to three nouns (Wave, Run, Session), and Postgres, `lfq`, the Docker `lfd` path, the conversations subsystem, Linear/Notion, and studio auth all left the tree. A net deletion, self-hosted by default.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Version `0.9.12` → `0.10.0` in `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`\n- `RELEASE_NOTES.md` rewritten for v0.10.0 (Wave primitive, three-noun runtime, self-hosted-by-default, Concerto rename to Loopflow.app, release/CI machinery, breaking-change operational notes)\n- Archived the release under `release/v0.10.0/`: `DECISIONS.md` moved from `release/unreleased/`, and a new `NOTES.md` snapshot added",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 139,
      "deletions": 20,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 805,
      "title": "provider_auth: fall back to Doppler for OAuth client credentials",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Asana OAuth now works with just Doppler configured — no env exports needed\ndoppler run -- lf op pm ...\n```\n\nOAuth client credentials (Asana and any PM provider) now resolve from the environment first, then fall back to fetching from Doppler when the env vars are unset. Previously, missing `ASANA_CLIENT_ID`/`ASANA_CLIENT_SECRET` (or the equivalent PM provider vars) failed hard with a `CommandUnavailable` error; now the broker shells out to `doppler secrets get <NAME> --plain` before giving up. The error message when both sources miss now mentions Doppler as an option.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `oauth_client_credentials` is now async and threads a Doppler fetch through `read_oauth_client_credential`, which tries env then the secret runner.\n- New `fetch_doppler_secret` runs `doppler secrets get --plain`; the value is consumed inline and never printed.\n- Callers (`start_auth`, `complete_auth`, `refresh_pm_oauth_token`, `oauth_app`) updated to await the now-async credential lookup.\n- Extracted `read_nonempty_value` shared by env and Doppler paths.\n- Tests inject a fake Doppler runner via `oauth_client_credentials_with_doppler_runner` and cover env-preferred, Doppler-fallback, and both-missing cases, with an `EnvGuard`/`env_lock` to serialize env mutation.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 205,
      "deletions": 20,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 806,
      "title": "wave: link concerto to asana, retire root/mobile/workflows waves",
      "body": "## What changed\n\nWires the Concerto wave into Asana PM sync and retires three waves whose roles have been absorbed elsewhere.\n\n```diff\n wave/concerto/GOAL.md  |  3 +++\n wave/mobile/GOAL.md    | 16 ---\n wave/root/*            | 122 ---\n wave/workflows/GOAL.md | 24 ---\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nConcerto's `GOAL.md` gains a `pm` block pointing at its Asana project, so its loop can sync against the same PM backend the other active waves use. The `root`, `mobile`, and `workflows` waves are deleted — `root` was the conductor/garden wave, `mobile` was already archived and dormant, and `workflows` owned the engine machinery. Removing their `GOAL.md`/`README`/`backlog`/`yaml` files trims the wave map down to the waves that are actually looping, keeping scope legible and free of stale ownership claims.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `wave/concerto/GOAL.md`: add `pm.provider: asana` + `asana_project` id\n- Delete `wave/root/` (GOAL.md, README.md, backlog.md, root.yaml)\n- Delete `wave/mobile/GOAL.md` (archived, no active surface)\n- Delete `wave/workflows/GOAL.md`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3,
      "deletions": 162,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 807,
      "title": "wave: retire the website wave wrapper",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nRemoves the Website wave's steering files (`wave/website/GOAL.md` and `wave/website/MEMORY.md`), retiring the wave wrapper. This follows the earlier retirement of the root/mobile/workflows waves and the linking of concerto to asana — the website wave's orchestration harness is no longer needed to keep the public docs and site in lockstep with the library.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Delete `wave/website/GOAL.md` — the ship-roadmap loop prompt and metrics for truing-up the public site\n- Delete `wave/website/MEMORY.md` — the wave's shipped/model/next memory (site import, one-docs-source, fly.io deploy)\n\nNet: 43 lines removed, no product code touched.",
